This is a classic **small file problem** — the bottleneck is not bandwidth, it's **request overhead per file**. Here's what's happening:

---

## Why `s3 sync` is Slow for Small Files

```
Big file (tgz):
  1 request → multipart upload → saturates pipe → ~100 MB/s ✓

Small files (100KB each):
  1 file = 1 PUT request
  100K files/folder = 100K round trips
  Each round trip: DNS + TCP + TLS + S3 auth + metadata = ~50-200ms latency
  
  5 workers × 200 concurrent req = 1000 req/s
  1000 req/s × 100KB = 100 MB/s theoretical
  BUT: S3 request rate limits + TLS overhead = ~5 MB/s actual ❌
```

**The real killers:**
| Cause | Impact |
|---|---|
| Per-file TLS handshake overhead | High CPU, kills throughput |
| S3 `PUT` rate limit per prefix | Default 3500 PUT/s per prefix |
| Sequential DNS/TCP per connection | Latency stacks up |
| `aws s3 sync` lists before uploading | Extra LIST API calls |
| Small payload = high metadata ratio | 100KB file, 10KB HTTP headers = 10% waste |

---

## Solution 1: Fix the S3 Prefix (Biggest Win)

S3 rate limits are **per prefix**. All files in one folder = one prefix = 3500 PUT/s cap.

```ksh
# BAD - all 100K files hit same prefix limit:
# s3://bucket/folder_001/file00001.dat  ← all same prefix
# s3://bucket/folder_001/file00002.dat

# GOOD - hash-shard the prefix to parallelize across S3 partitions:
# s3://bucket/ab/folder_001/file00001.dat
# s3://bucket/cd/folder_001/file00002.dat
# s3://bucket/ef/folder_001/file00003.dat
```

```ksh
#!/usr/bin/ksh
upload_with_sharding() {
    typeset src_folder="$1"
    typeset folder_name="${src_folder##*/}"

    find "$src_folder" -type f | while read filepath; do
        filename="${filepath##*/}"
        # Use first 2 chars of md5 as shard prefix
        shard=$(echo "$filename" | md5sum | cut -c1-2)
        aws s3 cp "$filepath" \
            "s3://bucket/${shard}/${folder_name}/${filename}" \
            --no-progress &
    done
    wait
}
```

> With 256 hex prefixes, each gets ~14 PUT/s of your 3500/s budget → **~3500 effective PUT/s total**

