## Solution 4: `aws s3api put-object` with GNU Parallel batching

Skip the Python SDK overhead, go raw:

```ksh
#!/usr/bin/ksh
BUCKET="your-bucket"
FOLDER="/data/folders/folder_001"
S3_PREFIX="folder_001"

# Feed files to parallel put-object at 500 concurrent
find "$FOLDER" -type f | \
    xargs -P 500 -I {} ksh -c '
        f="{}"
        key="'"$S3_PREFIX"'/${f##*/}"
        aws s3api put-object \
            --bucket "'"$BUCKET"'" \
            --key "$key" \
            --body "$f" \
            --no-cli-pager \
            >/dev/null 2>&1
    '
```

---

## Summary: What to Use

```
Goal                          Solution
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Max throughput, can restore   tar stream per folder → S3   ← BEST
Must have individual files    Fix prefix sharding + tune max_concurrent_requests=1000
Must use s3 sync              max_concurrent_requests=1000 + more parallel workers
```

**Bottom line:** `aws s3 sync` with small files will never match a streaming tar upload because you're paying per-file TCP/TLS/S3-auth overhead 100,000 times per folder. The tar stream amortizes all that cost across one multipart upload.
